shadowfetch-fireline (4.1.0-1) umbra; urgency=medium

  * BREAKING: both network postures now unshare the network. Posture 'allow'
    created no network namespace at all, so a sandbox that merely needed the
    internet kept the host's loopback services, the host's abstract AF_UNIX
    namespace and every LAN service the desktop user could reach. It now
    reaches the outside through a slirp4netns NAT attached to its own
    namespace with --disable-host-loopback: the internet, and not the host.
    An agent that relied on reaching a service on your 127.0.0.1 no longer
    can.
  * BREAKING: a networked run is REFUSED when no slirp4netns exists at a
    trusted absolute path, rather than quietly falling back to sharing the
    host network -- the fallback would hand back exactly the containment the
    session asked for. A sandbox that does not report its namespace is
    refused with that sentence.
  * BREAKING: the Fireline polkit action is removed. It authorised a generic
    shell escalation to root.
  * BREAKING: the fs MCP server fails closed without an explicit scope
    instead of choosing one.
  * A declared egress allowlist is now enforced as a default-DROP nftables
    ruleset installed inside the sandbox's own network namespace. Measured
    through the real Firebreak: the allowlisted address reached, an
    un-allowlisted address blocked, net=none blocks both, and the host
    loopback and abstract sockets stay unreachable in both postures.
  * Add a seccomp-BPF filter denying 46 syscalls, among them ptrace,
    process_vm_readv, process_vm_writev, the io_uring family, mount, setns,
    the module and kexec calls, perf_event_open and bpf. The assembled
    program is self-tested against this kernel before the run starts, and a
    kernel that refuses it refuses the run rather than degrading it.
  * Names resolve inside a networked sandbox for the first time. The bound
    /etc/resolv.conf names 127.0.0.53, which in the sandbox's own namespace
    is its own empty loopback, so every cloud provider failed at getaddrinfo
    while an IP address was reachable the whole time. The networked posture
    binds a resolver pointing at the NAT's forwarder, and the cost -- a
    query name can carry data out -- is stated in the enforcement mechanism
    rather than hidden.
  * masked_paths are real mounts rather than a recorded declaration.
    --mask-path was accepted and applied by nothing; its own help text said
    RECORDED ONLY, so a provider could declare that .env and a workspace's
    private keys were masked, the receipt printed the declaration as fact,
    and the agent read every one of them. An empty tmpfs now hides a
    directory and /dev/null overlays a file, applied after the workspace
    bind. Direct opens, absolute paths, relative traversal, symlinks, nested
    keys, directory listings and renaming the target are all denied where
    the previous build read the secret.
  * The launch chain no longer inherits the caller's environment. A
    PYTHONPATH pointing at a sitecustomize.py ran attacker code inside the
    namespace helper BEFORE nft installed the ruleset, blanked the ruleset,
    and the sandbox then reached 8.8.8.8 and the LAN with an allowlist
    naming neither -- while the record said enforced. The helper interpreter
    runs -I -S, the shebang stopped asking PATH which python to be, and
    Firebreak re-execs isolated when the environment is hostile.
  * Firebreak's readiness verdict and its checkpoint binary, and sf_mcp's
    Passport lookup -- an attestation of the machine's posture that an agent
    reads back -- resolve to trusted absolute paths instead of through the
    inherited PATH.
  * shadowfetch-checkpoint gains a structured interface, so callers stop
    parsing its prose, and undo is transactional: restore to a sibling,
    verify path by path and byte by byte, atomically exchange, then confirm
    by (st_dev, st_ino) that the directory now answering to the workspace's
    name is the one that was verified. A partial recovery is never reported
    as success.
  * Record Firebreak sessions, and audit MCP tool calls.
  * Run the containment acceptance suite in `make test`.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 18:28:58 -0400

